Q: CodeIgniter - constructors in models A: <blockquote>
<p>What I want to do is pass the posted form ($_POST) to my model, then
do stuff with it. But it seems messy to pass $_POST to each method of
my model.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I fundamentally disagree.  It is not messy to explicitly isolate subsets of submission data which are specifically relevant to each model method.</p>
<p>A layer outside of the model should be responsible for validating and/or sanitizing submission payloads. Model methods should not be expected to prepare/manipulate data prior to interacting with databases.</p>
<p>For re-usability and ease of unit testing, model methods should not directly access submission data or session data. All required data should be passed in as parameters. All method parameters and returns should have narrowed data type declarations (broad allowances like <code>mixed</code> doesn't typically help the maintainability of the application).</p>
<hr />
<p>As someone not familiar with your application, I have no idea what data would be available in the model's  <code>search_result</code> property or let's assume you meant <code>search_query</code>. A modern trend is to use a DTO (data transfer object) so that there is better control of the structure and typing within the payload. Alternatively, populate individual properties.</p>
<p>I will say that CodeIgniter's <code>$this-&gt;input-&gt;post()</code> conveniently acknowledges a whitelist array of field names (which can be declared statically or dynamically).</p>
<p>If you must continue down this road, below is a dynamic way to load individually typed model object properties from the controller's constructor.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>class Search extends CI_Controller
{
    public function __construct()
    {
        parent::__construct();
        $this-&gt;load-&gt;model('Search_model', 'SearchModel');
        $whitelist = get_object_vars($this-&gt;SearchModel);
        foreach ($this-&gt;input-&gt;post($whitelist) as $prop =&gt; $val) {
            $this-&gt;SearchModel-&gt;$prop = $val;
        }
    }

    public function byCat()
    {
        $this-&gt;load-&gt;view(
            'search_results',
            ['results' =&gt; $this-&gt;SearchModel-&gt;searchByCategory()]
        );
    }
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>class Search_model extends CI_Model
{
    public ?int $productId = null;
    public ?string $productName = null;
    public ?string $category = null;
    public ?string $datetime = null;
    public ?int $userId = null;

    public function __construct()
    {
        parent::__construct();
        $this-&gt;load-&gt;database();
    }

    public function searchByCategory(): array
    {
        return $this-&gt;db-&gt;get_where('products', ['category' =&gt; $this-&gt;category])-&gt;result();
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Honestly though, I wouldn't design a model as above in a professional project because I don't find the implicit magic to be beneficial. Here's <a href="https://3v4l.org/7opJb" rel="nofollow noreferrer">a modified demo of the above</a>.</p>

Q: WPF (.NET 8) XAML cannot resolve namespaces, converters, ViewModels despite classes being public A: <ol>
<li>Most errors are related to the presence of the &quot;AssemblyInfo.cs&quot; file. Please exclude it from your project.</li>
</ol>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/wVm1CsY8.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/wVm1CsY8.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<ol start="2">
<li>The next error is that you defined the key names like this:
<a href="https://i.sstatic.net/AJzoqRG8.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/AJzoqRG8.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></li>
</ol>
<p>But you are trying to access them using different keys:
<a href="https://i.sstatic.net/9amd2SKN.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/9amd2SKN.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>The correct way to reference them is like this:
<a href="https://i.sstatic.net/M68DCJvp.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/M68DCJvp.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>If you prefer using shorter keys, you should define them when declaring the resource:
<a href="https://i.sstatic.net/3KKxNmSl.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/3KKxNmSl.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<ol start="3">
<li><p>The &quot;DatabaseService.cs&quot; file does not contain the &quot;using System.IO;&quot;</p>
</li>
<li><p>The &quot;OllamaService.StreamChatAsync(...)&quot; method is implemented incorrectly.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Your code looks like this:<a href="https://i.sstatic.net/ykafNA0w.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/ykafNA0w.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>But it should be like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>        HttpResponseMessage response;
        string? error;
        try
        {
            response = await _http.SendAsync(req, HttpCompletionOption.ResponseHeadersRead, ct);
            error = null;
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            error = $&quot;\n\n⚠️ **Connection Error**: {ex.Message}\n\nMake sure Ollama is running: `ollama serve`&quot;;
            response = null!;
        }
        if (error is not null)
        {
            yield return error;
            yield break;
        }
</code></pre>
<ol start="5">
<li>I couldn't find in your code which converter you wanted to specify under the &quot;BoolGrid&quot; key:
<a href="https://i.sstatic.net/oTsjhFFA.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/oTsjhFFA.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></li>
</ol>
<p>Replaced for the test with a fixed size:</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>        &lt;Grid Background=&quot;{StaticResource BgDeepBrush}&quot;&gt;
            &lt;Grid.ColumnDefinitions&gt;
                &lt;!-- Sidebar --&gt;
                &lt;ColumnDefinition Width=&quot;200&quot;/&gt;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Result:</strong> After these changes, the project began to build and run normally.</p>
<p>I've created a Pull Request that addresses the current project launching issues. All fixes are included.</p>

Q: Levenshtein distance and Jaro–Winkler similarity A: <pre><code>
## The Problem: Performance

### Class Under Analysis: `CorporateDuplicateFinder`

Located at: [`src/main/java/com/complianx/matching/CorporateDuplicateFinder.java`](src/main/java/com/complianx/matching/CorporateDuplicateFinder.java)

The `findDuplicates()` method (lines 13-31) contains the core performance issue:

```java
public List&lt;MatchResult&gt; findDuplicates(List&lt;String&gt; companyNames) {
    List&lt;MatchResult&gt; matches = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

    // Compare each company name with every other company name
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; companyNames.size(); i++) {
        for (int j = i + 1; j &lt; companyNames.size(); j++) {
            String name1 = companyNames.get(i);
            String name2 = companyNames.get(j);

            Double score = corporateNameMatcher.matchCorporateNames(name1, name2);

            if (score != null &amp;&amp; score &gt; 0.85) {
                matches.add(new MatchResult(name1, name2, score));
            }
        }
    }

    return matches;
}
</code></pre>
<h3>Why This Is Problematic</h3>
<p>The nested loop creates <strong>O(n²) time complexity</strong>, where <code>n</code> is the number of company names. For each pair of names, the algorithm calls <code>matchCorporateNames()</code>, which performs multiple expensive string operations.</p>
<div class="s-table-container"><table class="s-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Dataset</th>
<th>Companies</th>
<th>Comparisons</th>
<th>Feasibility</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Small</td>
<td>~500</td>
<td>~125,000</td>
<td>Completes in seconds</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Large</td>
<td>~244,000</td>
<td>~29.8 billion</td>
<td><strong>Will not complete</strong></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The matching algorithm itself (<code>CorporateNameMatcher.matchCorporateNames()</code>) is complex and computationally expensive per call. With nearly 30 billion comparisons needed for the large dataset, the current implementation is simply not viable</p>
